# How to Set Base URL in Yii Framework

You might encounter a situation where you need to construct absolute URLs for assets, redirects, or API calls, only to find that `Yii::app()->request->baseUrl` returns an empty string. This behavior is standard within the Yii framework; the base URL is not populated by default because the framework expects you to explicitly define it during application configuration. Without this definition, Yii cannot determine where your application resides relative to the web root, leading to incomplete paths like `/assets/style.css` instead of `https://example.com/assets/style.css`.

To resolve this, you must configure the `baseUrl` property in your application bootstrap or configuration file. The most common approach involves modifying the `web.php` configuration file found in the `config` directory. You need to set the `components['request']['baseUrl']` property to the desired path.

```php
// In config/web.php
return [
    'components' => [
        'request' => [
            // Set base URL relative to the web root
            'baseUrl' => '/my-application', 
            // Ensure scriptUrl is also set if you are serving from a subdirectory
            'scriptUrl' => '/my-application/index.php', 
        ],
    ],
];
```

When setting this value, it is crucial to understand what constitutes the "base URL." It represents the directory name of your application relative to the domain root. If your application lives at `https://example.com/my-app`, the `baseUrl` should be `/my-app`. Note that trailing slashes are generally discouraged in configuration values unless specifically required by a component, as they can sometimes cause double-slash issues when concatenated with relative paths.

The `scriptUrl` property is often paired with `baseUrl` but serves a distinct purpose. While `baseUrl` defines the directory structure for assets and routing, `scriptUrl` points directly to the entry script (usually `index.php`). If you are running Yii without a dedicated entry script or from a different location than your web root, misconfiguring these two values can result in 404 errors when loading layouts or generating URLs.

If you are deploying a multi-tenant application where each tenant has a different subdomain but shares the same codebase, relying solely on `baseUrl` defined statically might not be dynamic enough. In such scenarios, developers often prefer to set the base URL based on the request host. However, Yii provides utility methods in `CUrlManager` (in older versions) or `yii\web\UrlRule` that can help manage this logic without hardcoding paths everywhere.

Another consideration is the difference between setting the base URL for asset management versus routing. When using `yii\web\AssetManager`, ensuring the `baseUrl` is correctly set prevents the framework from trying to locate assets relative to a non-existent directory. If you are upgrading from an older Yii version (1.x) to 2.x or 3.x, be aware that the application architecture has changed significantly. In Yii 2 and 3, `Yii::app()` is deprecated in favor of dependency injection via the container, so you would access the request object differently:

```php
// Yii 2 / 3 compatible approach using the container
return \Yii::$container->get('yii\web\Request')->baseUrl;
```

Ultimately, defining `baseUrl` is a one-time configuration task that prevents runtime errors related to asset loading and URL generation. If you find yourself constantly dealing with relative paths that break in production environments, investing time in configuring this correctly during the bootstrap phase saves significant debugging hours later. For teams migrating from other frameworks like Laravel, where base paths are often inferred from `APP_URL` environment variables, remember that Yii requires this explicit configuration step to function as intended.
